<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Egypt, North Africa and Sudan</p><h1 class="st-title">Emirate of Adrar</h1><p class="st-meta">1740 CE – 1909 CE (approx.) · capital Atar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Hassani emirate on the Adrar plateau of Mauritania, controlling the caravan towns of Atar, Chinguetti and Ouadane and the salt of Ijil.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> formed in the mid-18th century when a Hassani warrior lineage, the Awlad Yahya bin Uthman, established dominance over the Adrar plateau of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritania">Mauritania</a>. It took over oases long settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> scholarly and trading communities, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atar">Atar</a> emerging as its main centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> taxed the date oases and caravan towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinguetti">Chinguetti</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ouadane">Ouadane</a>, famous for their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> libraries, and controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-trade">salt trade</a> from the Ijil mines. Society was divided between Hassani warriors, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zawaya">Zawaya</a> scholars, tributaries and enslaved people. Succession disputes and feuds between branches of the ruling family were frequent.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>French forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-gouraud">Henri Gouraud</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atar">Atar</a> in 1909, overcoming resistance encouraged by the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-al-aynayn">Ma al-Aynayn</a>. Adrar became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-mauritania">French Mauritania</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> was absorbed into colonial administration.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="emirate, 1740 CE – 1909 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40